Yes. There are three different ideas there, and they lock together.

Role-play is controlled ego permeability. You temporarily stop insisting that every behavior has to certify “who I really am” and let yourself inhabit a character, stance, mood, or relational role. That can make play, intimacy, empathy, leadership, creativity, even learning much easier. The important part is that the costume is reversible: embody it fully without confusing it for the entirety of you.

And “show her how you like to play” is stronger than hoping someone infers it. Intimacy has an information problem. If the private game never leaves your head, another person only gets the shadows of it. Opening the channel lets them respond, accept, modify, reciprocate, or decline. That creates an actual shared game instead of two people guessing at each other.

Your body-language point is the same agency principle one level down. Body language is telemetry, not necessarily command. A racing heart can report fear without issuing “retreat.” A slumped posture can report exhaustion without defining your identity. You can listen without obeying:

signal → interpretation → choice, rather than signal → automatic behavior.

Then your exceptional/common cycle is bigger.

Something works unusually well → it looks exceptional → others copy it → infrastructure forms around it → it becomes accessible → eventually it becomes ordinary.

exception → demonstration → diffusion → normalization → standard

That is how a lot of progress succeeds. The electric light stops being miraculous precisely because electricity won. GPS stops being extraordinary because everyone carries it. A once-elite capability becomes a button.

So making uncommonness itself the incentive creates a pathological treadmill: as soon as everybody gains the good thing, its status value disappears and people have to manufacture a new scarcity.

Better objective function:

reward what works, even when success makes it common.

And your last distinction is especially good:

what works ≠ what makes us work.

A system can reward difficulty, hours, suffering, exclusivity, credentialing, effort-signaling—things that make people work—while accidentally punishing the invention that makes the work unnecessary.

If someone builds a machine that turns an eight-hour problem into a button press, the correct response isn’t:

“But you didn’t work hard enough.”

The disappearance of unnecessary labor is the product.

That makes the cycle almost evolutionary:

chosen → exceptional → copied → common → invisible infrastructure → new frontier

The exceptional should aspire to become common.

Otherwise we’re not raising the floor. We’re just defending the pedestal.